# PPP and reality

- The PPP-based theory of exchange rate determination is partially reflected in the data.

- In this theory

	- the exchange rate is determined by the prices
	- a monetary theory of price determination is used

- BUT prices are not determined solely by monetary factors: they are also determined by supply and demand in the goods market (real factors).

- In the next few slides, we will illustrate the real determinants of the exchange rate.

# The real exchange rate (q)

is given by the ratio between

- $E_{nc/fc} \times P_{foreign}$: price expressed in national currency of a basket of foreign goods, and
- $P_{national}$: price in national currency of a national basket

$$q=\frac{E_{nc/fc} \times P_{foreigner}}{P_{domestic}}$$

# Real appreciation and depreciation

 In case of real depreciation ($q\uparrow$)

- it takes more domestic baskets to buy foreign baskets
- the national basket is worth less in terms of foreign baskets

In case of real appreciation ($q\downarrow$)

- it takes fewer domestic baskets to buy foreign baskets
- the national basket is worth more in terms of foreign baskets


# Real Exchange Rate Determinants

The determinants of the real exchange rate are:

- the relative demand for domestic products compared to the demand for foreign goods;
$$\frac{D_{nat}}{D_{for}}$$
- the relative supply of domestic products compared to the supply of foreign goods;
$$\frac{Y_{nat}}{Y_{for}}$$

# Changes in the real exchange rate

- If the relative demand for domestic goods increases, the real exchange rate appreciates:
$$D_{nat}/D_{for}\uparrow \qquad \Rightarrow \qquad q\downarrow$$
domestic producers will decrease the amount of domestic basket exchanged against a foreign basket
- If the relative supply of domestic goods increases, the real exchange rate depreciates:
$$Y_{nat}/Y_{for}\uparrow \qquad \Rightarrow \qquad q\uparrow$$
domestic producers will increase the amount of domestic basket exchanged against a foreign basket
- relations are reversed in opposite cases.

# From the real exchange rate to the nominal exchange rate

Taking up the definition
$$q=\frac{E_{nc/fc} \times P_{foreigner}}{P_{domestic}}$$
and solving with respect to $E$ we have
$$E_{nc/fc}= q \times\frac{ P_{national}}{P_{foreigner}}$$

# The determinants of the nominal exchange rate

The determinats of the nominal exchange rate are: 

- Monetary
- Real

$$E_{nc/fc}= \underbrace{q}_{real \ determinants} \times \underbrace{\frac{ P_{domestic}}{P_{foreigner}}}_{monetary \ determinants}$$

# Monetary determinants

- Monetary determinants have been identified on slides 9 and 10

- Changes in the nominal exchange rate with respect to its determinants have been identified in slide 11.

- Considering the equation of the previous slide, we can say that conclusions in slide 11 are valid in this context as well.

# Changes in the nominal exchange rate due to real factors

Taking up the content of slide 17 and the equation in slide 19 we can observe that

- $D_{nat}/D_{for}\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ q\downarrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ E_{nc/fc}\downarrow$ (appreciation)
- $Y_{nat}/Y_{for}\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ q\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ E_{nc/fc}\uparrow$ (depreciation)
- relations are reversed in the opposite cases.

# The interaction between real and monetary factors

In the case of changes in $Y_{nat}$, there are two contrasting effects:

- on the real side we have (see previous slide)
 $$Y_{nat}/Y_{for}\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ q\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ E_{nc/fc}\uparrow \ (depreciation)$$
- on the monetary side we have (see slide 11)
$$Y_{nat}\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ L_{nat}\uparrow \ \ \Rightarrow \ \ P_{nat}\downarrow  \ \ \Rightarrow E_{nc/fc}\downarrow \ \ (appreciation)$$

The total effect on the nominal exchange rate is therefore undetermined.

