# PPP in relative terms

- PPP in relative terms is obtained from PPP in absolute terms, making the transition to rates of change.
- There are several ways to get a good approximation of the rate of change 
	- see note 1 in section 5.2 of the textbook
	- or by making the derivative of the logarithm of the variable with respect to time 
- In these slides, we'll use the following results:
	- when a variable is the product of two other variables, its rate of change is the sum of the rates of change of the two variables in the product
	- when a variable is the quotient of two variables, its rate of change is the difference of the rates of change of the variables in the ratio

# Examples

Recall that the rate of change of a variable $X$ is
$$\frac{X_t-X_{t-1}}{X_{t-1}}$$

- the rate of change of $P_{nat}=E\times P_{for}$ is approximated by
$$\frac{P_{nat,t}-P_{nat,t-1}}{P_{nat,t-1}}=\frac{E_t-E_{t-1}}{E_{t-1}}+\frac{P_{for,t}-P_{for,t-1}}{P_{for,t-1}}$$
- the rate of change of $E=\frac{P_{nat}}{P_{for}}$ is approximated by
$$\frac{E_t-E_{t-1}}{E_{t-1}}=\frac{P_{nat,t}-P_{nat,t-1}}{P_{nat,t-1}}-\frac{P_{for,t}-P_{for,t-1}}{P_{for,t-1}}$$

#

- The rate of change of prices in economics is called inflation (or deflation if negative), and in these slides, it is referred to as $\pi$

- The last equation of the previous slide can therefore be expressed as equation 5.2 of the textbook
$$\frac{E_t-E_{t-1}}{E_{t-1}}=\pi_{nat,t}-\pi_{for,t}$$

- this is the equation of PPP in relative terms.

# Benefits of PPP in Relative Terms

- PPP in relative terms may hold even if PPP in absolute terms is not.
- In particular, it can also hold if the general price level is calculated using baskets with different compositions.

# Monetary policy in dynamic models

- Monetary policy is typically implemented by varying the amount of money by a certain percentage over each period.

- Monetary policy decisions therefore consist of movements in the rate of change in the quantity of money.

- It should be remembered that with the hypothesis of completely flexible prices, a certain rate of change in the quantity of money generates an equal rate of change in the price level.

- In other words, inflation is equal to the rate of change in the quantity of money.

# Expectations in dynamic models

- In dynamic models, the perspective aspect and therefore expectations take on importance.

- We can then move to the prospective version of PPP in relative terms
$$\frac{E^e-E}{E}=\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{for}$$

# Inflation and interest rates

- By combining the prospective version of PPP in relative terms with interest rate parity, we can arrive at the relationship between interest rates and inflation rates.

$$\overbrace{R_{nat}-R_{for}=\frac{E^e-E}{E}}^{\textnormal{interest rate parity}}=\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{for}$$
\vskip-13.5mm
$$R_{nat}-R_{for}=\underbrace{\frac{E^e-E}{E}=\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{for}}_{\textnormal{relative PPP}}$$

we obtain
$$R_{nat}-R_{for}=\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{for}$$
i.e. the interest rate differential between two countries is determined by the inflation rate differential

# The Fisher Effect

\small
In persistent inflation situations, the nominal interest rate varies according to the inflation rate.

- The lender requires compensation in real terms, i.e. he requires a certain real interest rate ($r$).

- If there is inflation, the nominal interest rate will be set at a level that compensates for the loss of purchasing power due to inflation:
$$R=r+\pi^e$$

- From a prospective formulation we can define the real interest rate that will be obtained as: $r^e=R-\pi^e$

- the equation we arrived at in the previous slide can be expressed as:
$\underbrace{R_{nat}-\pi^e_{nat}}_{r^e_{nat}}=\underbrace{R_{for}-\pi^e_{for}}_{r^e_{for}}$

# Dynamics as a result of changes in monetary policy

Using the above notions, it is possible to show that an increase in the growth rate of money gives rise to the dynamics shown in Figure 5.1 of the textbook.

# Explanation of Figure 5.1

\label{pg:RandE}

- Increasing the rate of growth of money generates inflation expectations $\pi^e_{nat}\uparrow$
- with the same foreign inflation, this generates an expectation of depreciation of the exchange rate ($(E^e-E)/E=\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{for}$)
- The expectation of depreciation causes the interest rate to rise ($R_{nat}=R_{for}+(e^e-E)/E$). (This generates the jump in graph b of the figure)
- The rise in the interest rate implies a reduction in the demand for real money balances ($L$), and therefore in the price level ($P_{nat}=M^s_{nat}/L_{nat}$). (This generates the jump in graph c).
- The exchange rate dynamics follow the price dynamics for PPP ($E=P_{nat}/P_{for}$).

# Changes due to the real exchange rate

By enriching the scheme with the real exchange rate, we have
$$E=q\frac{P_{nat}}{P_{for}}$$
Moving to the rates of change, we have

$$\frac{E^e-E}{E}=\frac{q^e-q}{q}+\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{for}$$
Using interest rate parity, we have
$$R_{nat}-R_{for}=\frac{q^e-q}{q}+\pi^e_{nat}-\pi^e_{nat}$$
$$R_{nat}-\pi^e_{nat}-(R_{for}-\pi^e_{for})=\frac{q^e-q}{q}$$
$$r^e_{nat}-r^e_{for}=\frac{q^e-q}{q}$$

#

That is, the differential in expected real interest rates equals the depreciation of the real exchange rate.





